WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.702
If you look up how to do a spiral pass 
on the Internet —

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.071
which is something I had to do 
for this video

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.607
because I really suck at 
throwing footballs —

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.176
the main thing you'll see is that 
you need to position your fingers

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:13.346
in a way that'll make the ball spin 
as it flies through the air.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.416
I was breaking my arm 
trying to do this

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.752
so I could see a physics problem 
up close.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.421
And the problem is right... here.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:25.025
When you throw a good spiral pass, 
the ball starts pointed up

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.428
tips over mid-air and ends
pointed down.

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:31.231
We're used to seeing a football do this.

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:34.501
But according to physics, 
that's highly unusual.

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.803
When we throw objects, 
they are usually 

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:40.273
going to either retain the same angle 
as when they originally thrown

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:42.142
like this loaf of bread.

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:45.945
Or flip over themselves several times
until they land.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.781
Like this box of girl scout cookies.

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:51.651
So what is it about a football that 
makes it want to tip over?

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:56.723
I first got interested in this problem, 
I'm guessing 2000.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:00.794
We published the paper in 2020, 
so it really was 20 years.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:02.929
My name is Timothy Gay.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:05.365
I'm an atomic physicist by day

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:09.035
and my secret identity is 
football physicist at night.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.839
The best way to understand 
why the ball turns over

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:16.242
is to make an analogy 
with a spinning top.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:19.212
Spinning tops are like catnip 
for a physicist.

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.648
On its own, a top falls over.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:24.184
Give it top a spin
and it stands up.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.719
It balances itself around a line

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:30.256
a center point that physicists call
the reference axis.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:34.127
This axis is determined by the main force 
acting on the object

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.229
which in this case is gravity.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:38.431
Gravity is pulling straight down

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:40.934
and the top is fighting this by 
spinning around

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:44.170
wobbling around this vertical line,
this reference axis.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.839
A football is a top.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:47.941
You can spin it in the exact same way

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.243
and this is in part because of its shape.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.580
Both a football and a top are 
cylindrically symmetrical.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.750
A cylindrically symmetric object 
is one which if I rotate

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.453
you can't tell that I'm turning it.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:04.224
Objects which have this symmetry 
have a big impact

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:07.527
on how the object behaves 
when I apply forces to it.

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.362
So when you spin them...

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:12.532
they both wobble around 
a reference axis.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.068
Bending makes all the difference.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:17.170
But once you spin football in the air

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.473
it's reference axis gets 
a little more complicated.

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:22.909
When you throw a nice spiral pass

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:26.279
gravity is only acting as a secondary force 
on the ball.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.681
Gravity is trying to pull the ball down

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.551
usually making its path to the ground
look like this.

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:34.254
But the main force acting 
against the ball

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:36.623
is the air molecules that are 
pushing against it

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.057
as it flies through the air.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.928
The ball pushes against the air and 
the air pushes back against it.

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:45.965
But because gravity makes 
the ball's trajectory a parabola

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.903
the air pushing against the ball 
changes directions as it flies.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.604
When the ball is moving up

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.207
the molecules are acting as 
downward pressure.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:57.310
When it's coming down, the molecules 
are pushing up.

00:02:57.577 --> 00:02:59.646
And just like the spinning top

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:03.149
pointing against the downward force 
of gravity to stay vertical

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:04.684
the spinning football wants

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:07.320
to push against the main force 
acting against it.

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:10.523
The air molecules like the tap on the table,

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.525
the ball is going to want to wobble around

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.761
its center line, its reference axis.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:18.464
And in this case, that ends up being 
the ball's trajectory —

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.099
its path through the air.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.768
What the football wants to do

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:24.637
is exactly what the top wants 
to do on the table.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:28.841
It wants to circle its axis, 
wants to circle around

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:31.144
that force direction.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:32.979
But in the case of the top

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:34.914
the force of gravity never changes.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.249
It's always vertical.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:38.051
Whereas for the football

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.587
the axis of the force is changing.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.958
You can see all these forces at play 
in this slow-mo 3D recreation.

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:46.292
The ball takes off

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.728
pointing towards the air pushing against it

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.863
wobbling around its reference axis.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:54.000
It follows the trajectory 
gravity laid out for it

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.836
eventually reaching a point where 
it starts coming back down

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.906
at which point the air forces are 
pushing up against it

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.041
and the ball points against them.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:05.878
But in regular motion, it just looks like 
the ball is tipping over.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.148
If you throw a football 
without it spinning

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:12.485
it would follow a similar trajectory, 
but it wouldn't tip over.

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:16.322
And if you try to throw a spiral loaf of bread 
or some other object

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.790
it wouldn't tip over either.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.827
It only works on objects that are 
cylindrically symmetrical

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.363
like a football or a rugby ball.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.967
A very smart person once said to me

00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:30.570
"Everything is simple once explained."

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.406
It's a hard problem because, 
first of all

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.942
there are a lot of 
potential explanations

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:40.713
which seem enticing at the beginning
but turn out to be wrong.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:43.850
And unfortunately, you can't really
solve a problem

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:48.054
without using some relatively 
ophisticated mathematics.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:52.892
But the basic ideas are simple enough 
for anybody

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:55.528
that's interested and 
is paying attention

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:56.863
to understand.

